n\nFor the past three years Tomás has run our 3 a.m. milking shift on his own, five days a week. No one checks on him at that hour. He is responsible for the herd, the equipment and the milk from start to finish, and the work gets done right. In three years he has missed two shifts. I don't hand that shift to just anyone, and I have never had reason to regret giving it to him.\n\nTomás also took on more than his job required. He learned our herd management software and now uses it to flag sick cows for our veterinarian. Last winter he caught nine cases of mastitis early. Our vet estimates that this saved the farm about $8,000. Nobody asked him to do that. He saw how the data connected to the animals in front of him and made it part of his routine. That is the reason I think he belongs in your program. He already thinks like a technician, and formal training will give him the depth to go further.\n\nHe also recognizes his own weak spots and works on them. His spoken English is strong. On his own initiative he is taking a night class to bring his written English up to the same level. He did not wait for anyone to tell him to.\n\nI want to see Tomás succeed in this program. While he is enrolled, I will keep a part-time weekend position open for him at Hollis Dairy.\n\nI recommend him without reservation. If you would like to talk about him further, please call me at 555-0187.\n\nSincerely,\n\nMike Hollis\nFarm Manager, Hollis Dairy\n555-0187\n</final_document>\n\nThe letter fits on one page as written. Before it goes into the portal by February 1, a few things to check:\n\n- **Date:** I didn't put a date on it. Portal submissions are dated automatically, but add one if you'd like.\n- **His last name:** I only used \"Tomás.\" Add his full name, especially in the first sentence, so the letter gets matched to his application.\n- **The vet's figure:** I described the $8,000 as your vet's estimate. That's accurate, and it's more convincing coming from the vet than from you.